--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -35,3 +35,13 @@ the target; an object patch is merged member by member, where `null` removes a m
 value is merged recursively. Arrays are replaced whole, and a non-object target is treated as an empty
 object. As the format specifies, a `null` value cannot be set through a merge patch. Keys such as
 `__proto__` are handled as ordinary own members and never pollute prototypes. Never throws.
+
+### `createPatch(from, to)`
+
+Produces a JSON Patch (RFC 6902) that turns `from` into `to` when passed to `applyPatch`; paths are JSON
+Pointers (RFC 6901, with `~0`/`~1` escaping). The patch is minimal in spirit, not just correct: objects are
+compared member by member and arrays item by item, recursing into changed values, so a small change gives a
+small patch. An item added to or removed from an array is a single `add` or `remove` and its neighbours are
+untouched; a moved item is a `remove` plus an `add`, not a rewrite of the items in between. Equal documents
+(JSON equality, section 4.6) give `[]`; values of different types are replaced whole (the root with path
+`""`). Neither argument is modified and the patch shares no structure with them.
